TASK
1. STRUCTURE — the blueprint must contain, as ## headings: The decision; Executive summary; The engagement — context and scope; Where you are today; The opportunity; How this makes money; The product, module by module; What we'd build first; What success looks like. Flag any missing or empty. The decision's REQUIRED shape is: one unmistakable recommendation, a phased sequence (Phase 1 = a rules-based or manual pilot), and closing "Now:" / "Not yet:" verdicts — that phased shape is correct, never a finding. Flag the decision ONLY when it merely lists options without choosing, when its phases contradict each other, or when Phase 1 carries a predictive/AI component or name. A Phase 1 explicitly described as rules-based or manual is CORRECT — never flag it for being what it must be.
2. CONSISTENCY — the document must not contradict the layers: every module named in the document exists in the module list and vice versa; the scope obeys the register (a capability engagement never redesigns the whole business — and recommendations stay inside the stated geography); the executive summary's claims appear in the body. The document must never state the same fact two different ways (a settlement cycle given as both "2 days" and "monthly", two different volumes for the same operation), and a figure must keep ONE frequency everywhere — a weekly number reused as monthly is a high finding. The decision's Phase 1 pilot must never be described as predictive AI, risk scoring, or an "engine" — a Phase 1 wearing an AI name is a high finding. ONE CAPABILITY, ONE NAME: every module keeps its exact decomposition name in every mention — a synonymized or rebranded module name is a high finding. ONE GATE: every pilot success criterion in the document must match the pilot decision gate above verbatim — a competing threshold, a different reduction basis (relative vs percentage-point), or two distinct metrics conflated into one is a high finding. Procedures must not present an unbuilt capability as usable today: a procedure whose steps depend on a to-be-built module but whose phase is "current" (or missing) is a high finding. A procedure marked "future" for a module listed in "What we'd build first" is CONSISTENT (build order is sequence; FUTURE is availability) — flag a FUTURE procedure only when its text tells staff to run it today.
BLOCK RELEASE (high finding) for: unknown numbers, raw module IDs, forward dependencies (an earlier phase relying on a later-phase module), phase contradictions, invented KPIs, inconsistent monthly bases, placeholder language or client-facing technical syntax.
3. COVERAGE — the structured layers live ALONGSIDE the document (they render as their own sections and pages); their absence from the document text is CORRECT and never a finding. A coverage finding exists only when a layer's COUNT above is zero: no journey stages, no scoreboard rows, no risks, or no quick wins.

